# Product rules

- Hidden tags = settings names/group keys — not hardcoded AI lists. No `blacklist_tags` / `hide_ai`.
- **Hide content** (blur, not list exclusion): Settings → Tags → Hide content rules (`content_hide_rules`: id, label defaulting to Rule #N, tag_names, optional message; rules list and each rule are collapsible). Media with any of a rule’s tags is blurred on viewer/detail/scrape/related/duplicates until that rule id is in `content_hide_revealed_rule_ids` (persisted unlock). While locked, media detail also hides source URLs and tags. Viewer **Show hidden content** (after Show/Hide hidden tags): generic confirm then any rule messages. Tap blur: if matching locked rules have messages, only those dialogs; otherwise the generic **Show all of the hidden content?** confirm. In **Select** mode (Viewer, Downloaded previously, Add related files), tapping a blurred card selects it and does not open the reveal dialog. Current-page tag chips / sidebar list only tags from non-blurred files on that page (selected filter tags still stay visible). While any tag filter is active, content-hidden files are omitted from the media list (via exclude_tags for locked rule tag names) until their rules are revealed. Settings has **Hide again** per revealed rule and **Hide all content again**.
- Seeded `ai` group is membership/color only.
- Ratings: half-stars `0.5`…`5` (float `ratings.score`). Media detail: keys `1`–`5` set the whole-star score immediately; a second digit within 2s makes `N.5` when higher than the first (e.g. 4 then 5 → 4.5), keeps `N` when the same (5 then 5 → 5), or restarts on a lower digit. Detail shortcuts (←/→, F, ↑/↓, rating digits) listen in capture phase so they still fire when a `<video>` has focus; they skip real typing targets and Ctrl/Meta/Alt combos (`utils/keyboardTarget`, so Ctrl+F is find, not fullscreen). Detail chrome overlays bottom-left info (`File N of total` across the filtered library / session, scraped time / size) and bottom-right ← / Full view / → (slightly left of) Download, rotate 90° CCW, and ⋮ with Share… (OS share sheet via Web Share API on desktop/Android/iOS when available; prefetches the file when the menu opens so the tap keeps the user gesture; falls back to download with a Files/Downloads hint when share is unavailable, e.g. insecure LAN `http://`) / Copy (full-resolution file) / Save As… — overlays do not reserve layout space so the media stage fills the viewport (no bottom thumbnail strip yet). ←/→ cross page boundaries and keep the grid page in sync. Tilting the mouse wheel left or right, and ←/→ when no file is open, do the same previous/next step in detail or switch the grid page.
- Similar files: images/gifs use pHash. Videos sample four frames with ffmpeg and match identical only. Hamming 0–2 (capped by `phash_threshold`) = Identical (merge in Scrape panel and media detail). 3–threshold = Similar for pictures (keep/discard). SHA-256 skips the second file. `scrape_skip_identical` (Settings and the Scrape page, default off) skips a new download in the identical band and copies tags + URL onto the kept row. Hand-mark from media detail links one or more other files by local database id as identical or similar (earliest id stays primary). **Add related files** opens on the database-id form; **Select a file** opens the shared media grid and hides that form until Done selecting. The open file’s tags start selected, with Clear selected, Search groups, and Show hidden tags. Each page is six other files in one row across the picker width. Select marks the chosen files to the open one. Review cards use **Keep all** / **Keep selected** (checkboxes after the button); identical cards also **Merge into selected**. Discarding or merging a member clears its `duplicate_of_id` so Downloaded previously does not keep a stale similar/identical mark on the discarded row.
- Scrape: page visible by default (`hide_scrape_page` false); top **Source** defaults to **Local**. Gallery sites come only from installed adapters (`data/adapters/*/adapter.json`; tracked sample is `adapters/example/` only — other `adapters/*` gitignored; install via Scrape **Install …** / **Load adapters folder…** — not auto-installed on Run). Empty/missing catalog coerces Source to Local. Site sources keep **Scraper** (agent) and filters; **Local** links PC folders/files in place by default, or copies them into `media/` when `local_import_storage` is `copy` (Settings → Other → Scrape and the Local scrape page; default `link`; no tags on import; SHA-256 + pHash; Include subfolders default on; native OS pickers). Chosen folders/files and the recursive flag persist in `session_settings.json` and always show again on Local; **Sync folders** re-scans the saved folders for new media and, when finished, lists linked library rows whose files are gone from those folders, then asks whether to delete them from the app. Permanent delete removes the library row only for linked files. Local items can gain source URLs. **Delete from local folder** copies the file into the library (same as a scraped file, keeping those URLs and replacing a `loc_` id with the post id from the URL) and deletes only the folder original. Detail always labels the block **Source URLs** and shows that source number. A local file path is not listed there. Linked files serve via `GET /media/{id}/file`. multi-URL union (one URL filter accepts a newline- or comma-separated list, or add multiple URL rows); tag `and` / `or` mode; each Tag row has a free-text input (any site tag) plus the shared colored single-select picker (search + group filters) over library tags; date range filter is date+time (`datetime-local` → `from..to` ISO local); date-only sides still mean start/end of day; site mode requires Source + Scraper before Run; exclude tags use the shared collapsible tag search/group-filter picker with adjacent **Add hidden tags** (merges settings hide tags/groups via `expandHiddenTags`); **limit** counts successful downloads only (skips do not) and does not apply when any URL filter is used (all seeded URLs / listing results are attempted); when the gallery has no more unseen posts the scrape stops even if the limit is unmet; gallery and filter finds are appended to **Queue to scrape** and drained in that same run, Settings → Other → Scrape **Downloads at once** (`scrape_concurrency`, default 2, range 1–6) files at a time; the listing stops adding URLs once downloaded, in-flight, and queued cover the limit; **Run scrape** retries **Skipped** without increasing `total`, and a failed retry does not stop the job; new **Queue to scrape** entries increase `total` when drained; **Never auto-run scrape** defaults off; listing **sort** is applied on the gallery (expand Filters → Oldest/Latest) before the download quota so asc+limit takes oldest matches; **Downloaded previously** defaults to this session, optional persisted `scrape_downloads_show_all` lists library by `scraped_at`; page size `scrape_downloads_page_size` (multiples of 18); Select lives directly beside Search tags and remains visible (disabled only with no items); selection keeps across pages and clears when filters, sort, or page size change;multi-file Share packs under Chromium 10-file/50 MiB limits with a Share next dialog until batches are done (files over 50 MB download instead);  bulk bar can **Download**, **Share**, or **Add tags** to the selection including files picked on other pages (same on the Viewer Select bar). Scrape pages open through `scrape_browser` (`auto`|`obscura`|`playwright`): Auto uses Obscura when that binary is installed, otherwise Playwright Chromium. Browsers are scrape-only: while `hide_scrape_page` is on they are never checked, downloaded or updated, Settings hides the Scrape browser panel, and media detail hides Rescrape / Update tags from URLs. With the Scrape page on, missing browsers are offered (Scrape page notice + Settings Download), never auto-installed; installed ones get a launcher update check at most once a day when **Check for updates on start** is on (off by default on a metered connection).
- Scrape panels: sibling panels under the shared Source/filter form (not nested inside it). Show a panel only when it is the form's current Source. **Unsupported** still shows when it is incomplete (`processed < total`), has Queue to scrape URLs, or is running. Sources are `Local`, an installed site's display name, or `Unsupported` for raw/extension file saves. Each panel has its own **Run** / **Stop** / **Clear queues**. Skipped, Cancelled, and Queue to scrape each have their own **Clear**. **Retry** on Skipped (and on the error list when Skipped is empty) loads only that source's skipped and error files again. Then the same **Never auto-run scrape** checkbox as Settings (shared `never_auto_run_scrape`), status line (starts with the source label, then message, `processed/total`, downloaded, skipped, cancelled), and its own Skipped / Cancelled / Queue to scrape lists. There is still one live job process-wide: Run is enabled on the panel that matches the form's Source (it uses the form's filters; Local runs the chosen folders/files), or on **Unsupported** (retries only its queued/skipped files; no filters); Stop on any panel stops the running job; **Clear queues** clears only that source's three lists and is blocked only while that source's job runs. A list's own **Clear** removes only that list. Counts, Skipped, Cancelled and Queue stay per source when another source runs.
- Browser extension (`extension/`, one plain-JS MV3 build for Chrome + Opera, Load unpacked): it does not download gallery files itself. Site rules live in the adapter `extension` block (`page_matches`, `post_id_patterns`, `post_url_template`, `file_matches`) and `GET /extension/sites` returns them. The extension caches supported sites, syncs every 5 minutes (and when the open page is missing from that list, or you press the popup sync button), and re-checks before a supported-site download. The popup says **Site supported** or **Site not supported** (yellow when not). Shortcut bindings are changed from **Extension settings** in the popup (defaults Insert = file, Home = page; each action also has an optional second binding, empty by default). Capture accepts keyboard keys (including Ctrl/Shift/Alt/Meta chords and uncommon ones like BrowserBack / F13+) and mouse buttons (left/middle/right/back/forward); mouse, browser-nav keys, and modifier chords preventDefault so they do not navigate away or steal browser find. The icon is green on any page of a supported site and yellow elsewhere. The top-left corner dot is **red** while the open post is in any download queue (Queue to scrape / offline queue / currently downloading) and turns **blue** when that file is fully in the library; matching uses the **page URL** as the key (query kept; left-prefix match so a file URL queued from that page still lights the post tab). When the file shortcut queues one or more media file URLs that are not the page itself, the largest by bytes is kept and keyed to that page URL. A URL lives in only one queue: re-adding it moves it (from Skipped / another source / the same Queue) to the end of the target Queue. The top-right red busy dot still means a scrape job is running. The popup asks the app with a short timeout and keeps the last status if a request fails, instead of staying on Checking app. The popup scrape status line uses the current page's source panel (`Unsupported` off-site, otherwise that site's panel). On a supported post page the popup also shows a short media line: **In library** when saved, and/or **In …** naming each queue that holds it (Downloading, Queue to scrape, Skipped, Cancelled, Offline queue), with the source label when known. Right-click **Download this file** (only on a `file_matches` link or image) and **Download this page** (every matching post or picture on the page) send cleaned URLs. Each URL enters **Queue to scrape** (a post page as itself, a CDN preview as the post page); when drained, or appended to an already-running drain, it increases `total` and does not reset processed, downloaded, or skipped. With **Never auto-run scrape** on they wait until **Run scrape**. A post that already finished, failed, or was cancelled can be queued again. The popup Download buttons stay on a supported site while the app is closed. Those links wait in the extension and are sent when MediaViewer is running. Files that fail, are stopped, or are still open when the app closes stay in a stored Skipped list across restarts. Extension links and gallery finds go through **Queue to scrape**. **Never auto-run scrape** defaults off: with it off, a queued extension link may start downloading immediately; with it on, links wait in **Queue to scrape** (under Skipped) until **Run scrape**. Run scrape retries Skipped without adding them on top of the current total, and a failed retry does not stop the job. Queue to scrape increases that total. With an empty form, Run scrape only retries those lists and does not start a new gallery listing. Download this file on a post already saved at full quality reports that it is in the library; a smaller video encode is replaced when the original file exists. The popup on a site's front page runs the same filters as the Scrape page (`POST /extension/scrape`). Off a supported site, right-click **Download this file (site not supported)** sends the picture URL; the app saves it with no tags (`POST /extension/save-file`). **Insert** (default) queues this post (or the post link under the pointer on a listing); **Home** queues this post when the page is one post, and every supported URL on a listing. Shortcuts do not call `preventDefault`, so the key keeps its normal job; while focus is in a text field the extension skips that press so letters still type. They still fire when a video/audio (or a hidden player focus trap) has focus, including inside iframes (`all_frames` inject + MAIN-world bridge). Keys work without an Allow button. While the app is running, the extension polls scrape status about every 5 seconds and shows the same processed/total, downloaded, skipped, and cancelled counts as the Scrape page (icon tooltip, progress bar, and popup), including after the job finishes. Skipped on that line is the Skipped list only; cancelled URLs are counted beside it. While a job runs, the toolbar icon shows a red busy dot on the top-right. A failed file is listed under Skipped with its error text. Videos try `.mov1080.mp4`, `.mov720.mp4`, and `.mov480.mp4` when the original name 404s. The Scrape progress line is `processed/total`. `processed` counts every settled file (downloads, skips, errors, and cancels), so a finished run reads complete (for example 58/58, downloaded 57, skipped 1). `total` is every file in the run: downloads, the queue, skips, cancels, and errors (and unavailable/interrupted), including already-have, filter, and identical skips. The download limit is not the denominator. **Stop** moves every file still left onto Cancelled and those count in `processed`. Starting a run again takes retried skipped and error URLs back out of `processed` until they settle again; a later success, skip, error, or cancel counts each once. A file that previously failed and then downloads is removed from the Skipped list, from `errors`, and from stored unfinished leftovers. After a restart / Reload, progress counters and Downloaded previously (session list) clear: `processed` is 0, `total` is the leftover Queue to scrape + Skipped + Cancelled count per panel, and downloaded / similar / errors / message reset; the three queue lists themselves persist for retry. Starting a run counts that leftover queue once, so a finished run reads `processed/total` with the two numbers equal when nothing is still outstanding. The Scrape progress line shows `cancelled N` and a Cancelled URL list for the rest of the session after a Stop. **Clear queues** (next to Run/Stop, confirm, disabled while a job runs or when all three lists are empty; `POST /scrape/clear-queues`, 409 while running) empties Queue to scrape, Skipped, and Cancelled including `data/scrape_leftovers.json`, and leaves **Downloaded previously** alone. Extension routes are behind `require_scrape_page`.
- Viewer tag layout (`viewer_tag_layout`): `sidebar` (default, left column of current-page tags, 10 per group, stored `usage_count`, click toggles filter; sidebar fills viewport height with docked pagination) or `chips` (filter above the grid). Tag names decode HTML entities on display; scrape `normalize_tag_name` uses `html.unescape` so new rows store real characters (e.g. `'` not `&#039;`). Settings groups: **General** (Theme, Display), **Tags** (aliases, hidden tags, Hide content blur rules, Recalculate tag counts, tags per page options), **Viewer** (UI + viewer options including items per page options), **Other** (scrape and tag-order options).
- Card grids show a cached square JPEG fitted to the card (compact 240, normal 360, large 480) instead of the original file. The cache follows that file’s size and timestamp and is removed when the library row is deleted, so a reused id or an older copied file does not keep the previous card. Viewer chooses the size from the current card width. The open page is decoded first, then `PREVIEW_PAGE_RADIUS` pages after it (default 2, in `frontend/src/utils/previewPageWindow.ts`); that many previous pages stay decoded and anything further away is dropped from memory. The browser keeps preview HTTP responses about a day (`max-age=86400`), so returning to a page often hits that cache. A video on a card stays that still until hover or autoplay loads the file (including while Select is on). Detail still shows the original.
- Device-local layout (this browser only, `localStorage` `scrap_device_prefs`): Viewer / Tags / Scrape downloads **items per page**, **grid thumbnail size**, and **tag layout**. First visit on a device can seed from the shared server value once, then changes stay on that device (PC vs phone). Shared `app_settings` still holds option lists (`viewer_page_sizes`, `tags_page_sizes`), hide tags, scrape options, and other library prefs.
- Viewer full page: column count prefers even rows for the current `pageSize` (so a short last page keeps the same card size with empty trailing cells); may add columns to shrink cards slightly and avoid a leftover viewport scroll. Pagination stays docked at the bottom of the viewer (Previous / editable page number / Next; typing a page and Enter or blur jumps there, clamped to 1…total). Browse sessions use a frozen media-id `cursor` (rule34-style `?page=&cursor=`): page 1 without cursor establishes `max(id)`; later pages send that cursor so `id <= cursor` keeps the page stable when newer files arrive; page 1 / sort / filters / page-size clear the cursor. Random sort ignores cursor. Dropdown option lists are settings `viewer_page_sizes` (Viewer panel, default 12–96 step 12, range 1–200) and `tags_page_sizes` (Tags → Tags panel, default 25/50/100, range 1–500); Tags always appends **All** (`0`). Default selected Viewer size is `36`. List editors open via **Edit** (summary line beside the button); Save/Cancel/Esc; dirty Cancel/Esc confirms discard. Adding a size inserts in sorted order. Live page size is device-local; hide-hidden-tags and **Show discarded** still patch from each page. Removing a size clamps the saved selection to a remaining option. Media grids (viewer, Downloaded previously, Add related files) share **AND** / **OR** for selected tags (`tag_mode`, default AND). Search tags looks up the full library tag list, so a match can be chosen even when that tag is not on the current page; the list then shows files for those tags. A group filter with an empty tag search stays on tags from the current page. **Include all tags** on the group panel then lists every library tag in the selected groups.
- Untyped site tags → `tags_untyped_group` (`default`|`unknown`).
- Media detail tags: first 25 + dark **Show more (+N)** / **Show less** chips; `media_detail_tags_collapsed` default true; session remembers toggle.
- Tag select display (`tags_select_display_mode`): `highlight` | `front` | `panel` (default) — viewer filter + pickers; click chip to toggle (no ×). Tag pickers share collapsible search, match mode and group filters; each surface may add adjacent toolbar actions.
- Launch: detached `pythonw` via `Run.bat` (plain start) or `Run.bat restart` (kill then start; in-app Reload). Tracked `VERSION` (starts at `0.1`) vs local `data/installed_version`: mismatch (or missing stamp) stops a running app, forces pip + frontend rebuild + exe ensure, stamps the version, then starts; match keeps light ensures and, if healthy on port 8000, only opens the viewer. Same gate for `Run.bat` and `MediaViewer.exe`. Bump `VERSION` when recipients should rebuild after `git pull`. The first `Run.bat` dependency install writes `MediaViewer.exe` in the project folder (file description **MediaViewer**). That exe and Run do not restart a running app when versions match: if it is already up they only open the viewer (same as the tray **Open viewer** item) and do not add a second tray icon. Settings **Launch on startup** writes a Startup-folder shortcut to that exe (so Task Manager does not list `pythonw`) and removes the old Run-key entry. **Start minimized** (default on — tray/server only until Open viewer); **display_mode** `window` opens a native WebView2 shell (normal Windows title bar, no browser chrome) via `pywebview`, with Chrome/Edge `--app=` only as fallback. One window host at a time (`services/app_window.py`): it listens on a localhost socket (`data/app_window.json`, auth key) for `show` / `quit`; Open viewer (tray click, Run-while-running, `/app/open-viewer`) sends `show` and spawns only when no host answers; tray **Quit** and in-app **Exit** send `quit` and kill leftover hosts by command line (Reload / `Run.bat restart` keep the window). WebView2 profile persists in `data/webview_profile/`. Settings saves only call `applyDisplayMode` when **Display** mode itself changes; `browser` opens a normal tab; detail fullscreen control; **Media number** = source|db. **`Run.bat restart`** and in-app **Reload** write `data/.skip_open_viewer` (2‑minute TTL), set `SCRAP_SKIP_OPEN_VIEWER`, and pass `--no-open-viewer` so relaunch does not open a second viewer (including the "already running" race, and including a version-triggered restart during Reload). A normal **`Run.bat`** clears a stale skip flag so Run-while-running still opens the viewer. Reload runs `Run.bat restart` and reloads the tab only after `/api/health` reports a new `boot_id` (not the dying process).
- Outside apps and libraries (binaries, extra packages, tools not already installed with the venv) get install and update logic in the launcher (`Run.bat` / `launcher.py`). Do not assume they are already on PATH. Anything used only for scraping stays behind the Scrape page: while `hide_scrape_page` is on, do not probe, download, or update it, and do not show its settings or actions. Same pattern as scrape browsers — offer Download when the Scrape page is on, never auto-install a missing scrape-only tool, and refuse those routes with `require_scrape_page`. Tools the library uses with the Scrape page hidden (ffmpeg frame hashes) are not behind that gate, but they still need launch install logic.
- Important settings keys live in `app_settings` / `types/appSettings.ts` — keep them aligned.
